   "id": "546a647c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Home Credit Default Risk — Capstone Project\n",
    "## Stacking Ensemble (XGBoost + LightGBM + Random Forest) with an MLP Meta-Learner\n",
    "\n",
    "**Author:** Richard  \n",
    "**Problem type:** Binary classification (loan default prediction)  \n",
    "**Dataset:** [Home Credit Default Risk](https://www.kaggle.com/c/home-credit-default-risk) — 7 relational tables\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1. Objective\n",
    "Predict the probability that an applicant will default on a loan (`TARGET = 1`). Because defaulters are far\n",
    "costlier to miss than false alarms are to investigate, the model is optimised for **recall / F2 / ROC-AUC**\n",
    "rather than raw accuracy.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2. Key challenge — class imbalance\n",
    "The target is highly imbalanced (~**91% non-default / ~9% default**). Every model below applies an\n",
    "imbalance correction (`scale_pos_weight`, `class_weight='balanced'`, `is_unbalance=True`) and the final\n",
    "decision threshold is tuned on the precision–recall curve instead of the default 0.5.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 3. Pipeline overview\n",
    "1. **Load** the main application tables plus 6 auxiliary credit-history tables.\n",
    "2. **Clean** erroneous sentinel values and engineer missingness flags.\n",
    "3. **Feature-engineer** each auxiliary table and aggregate it to one row per client (`SK_ID_CURR`).\n",
    "4. **Merge** all aggregates into a single wide table (`df_main`, ~490 features).\n",
    "5. **Train base models** (XGBoost, LightGBM, Random Forest) with 5-fold Stratified CV to produce\n",
    "   out-of-fold (OOF) probabilities.\n",
    "6. **Train the MLP meta-learner** on the OOF probabilities (with SMOTE + threshold tuning).\n",
    "7. **Evaluate** on a held-out split using Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1, F2 and ROC-AUC.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 4. Target metrics\n",
    "`ROC-AUC > 0.800`  ·  `Recall > 0.700`  ·  `F2 > 0.470`\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Reviewer notes:** a few correctness issues found during the comment pass are flagged inline with\n",
    "> `# >>> NOTE` / `# >>> BUG` markers. The original code is left unchanged so results remain reproducible;\n",
    "> the suggested fixes are written as comments next to each issue.\n"
   ]
  },
